### Classification under GRMS

**SEC HIIP Claim:** The report classifies the Project Cape Area accumulation as *discovered*, and
states that *"application of any risk factor to HIIP does not equate HIIP with reserves or contingent
resources"*. It applies no further classification: no reserves, contingent or prospective categories,
no 1P/2P/3P or 1C/2C/3C designations, and no reporting standard cited by name.

**HIP-RA-X Evaluation:** That caveat marks the boundary of what a volumetric in-place estimate can
claim. Heat in place is the parent quantity, and classification applies to quantities that are
recoverable — which requires the recovery assessment above. Having performed it, the estimate can be
placed within the Geothermal Resource Management System, the geothermal adaptation of SPE-PRMS.

HIP-RA-X reports the classification alongside the physical results:

<pre>
<b>      ***GRMS CLASSIFICATION***</b>
      Resource Class:                {{ rec_resource_class }}
      Estimate Scenario:             {{ rec_estimate_scenario }}
      Cumulative Category:           {{ rec_cumulative_category }}
</pre>

Contingent Resources because the accumulation is discovered and this analysis establishes a
recoverable quantity without determining commerciality; Reserves would require that determination.
The cumulative category is derived from the resource class and estimate scenario rather than
declared, so the Best Estimate of Contingent Resources is reported as 2C. The filing's Low, Best and
High estimates correspond to the GRMS Low, Best and High estimate scenarios, and hence to 1C, 2C
and 3C.

Two designations are deliberately absent. Project maturity sub-class and economic status describe the
project rather than the resource — whether development is pending, on hold or approved, and whether
the economics have been demonstrated — and those are determinations for an evaluator with access to
the project, not conclusions an independent analysis of a public filing can draw. HIP-RA-X reports
only the designations declared, which keeps the boundary between the model's results and the
evaluator's judgement visible in the output itself.

